<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garden of the Hesperides</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Garden of the Hesperides was in Greek myth the garden of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> at the western edge of the world, where golden apples were guarded by the nymphs called Hesperides and the dragon Ladon. Fetching the apples was one of the labours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. Some ancient writers placed the garden near the Greek city of Euesperides, modern Benghazi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garden-of-the-hesperides.jpg" alt="Garden of the Hesperides" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Burne-Jones · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GardenHesperides_BurneJones.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Greek mythology the Hesperides are the nymphs who tend the garden in which grow the trees of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> that bear golden apples. They were also called the Atlantides, from their reputed father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a>. Their name means originating from Hesperos, the evening, which also shares a root with the English word west. Their parents, number and names vary between sources. They are listed as daughters of Night, of Atlas and Hesperis, of Phorcys and Ceto, or of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themis">Themis</a>. Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> gives three, Aigle, Erytheis and Hespere, while Apollodorus gives four, Aigle, Erytheia, Hesperia and Arethusa. Euripides calls them minstrel maids for their sweet song.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The garden is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>'s orchard in the far west, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a>, at the edge of the ocean of Oceanus. According to the legend, the goddess Gaia brought branches bearing golden apples as a wedding gift when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and Hera married, and Hera had them planted in her gardens, which extended as far as Mount Atlas. The Hesperides tended the grove, and Hera set the immortal, never-sleeping, hundred-headed dragon Ladon to guard the apples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> places the garden at Lixus in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and elsewhere says the Hesperides live on two islands in the Atlantic. Diodorus gives a variant in which the Hesperides had flocks of sheep so beautiful that the poets called them golden apples, guarded by a shepherd named Dracon.</p><p>Euesperides, modern Benghazi, might have mythological associations with the garden. Diodorus gives a variant in which the Hesperides had flocks of sheep so beautiful that the poets called them golden apples, guarded by a shepherd named Dracon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The eleventh Labour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> was to steal the apples. In one version Heracles tricked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> into fetching them by offering to hold up the heavens for a while, and then walked away with them; in another he killed Ladon. According to Apollodorus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurystheus">Eurystheus</a> gave the apples back to Heracles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> returned them to the garden. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a> came to the Hesperian plain the next day, and the Hesperides directed them to a spring that Heracles had struck from a rock near Lake Triton. Heracles appears sitting in bliss in the garden on Attic pottery, especially from the late fifth century BCE.</p><p>By Roman times the garden had dwindled to a poetic convention. It returned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> poetry, where Robert Greene, Shakespeare and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-milton">John Milton</a> mention it, and Robert Herrick's collection of verse, Hesperides, was published in 1647. Later it was thought the golden apples might have been oranges, and the Greek botanical name for citrus species is Hesperidoeidē.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hesperides” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its name recalls the <b>Garden of the Hesperides</b>, placed nearby by Greek legend.</blockquote><a href="/state/euesperides">Euesperides</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek writers placed the <b>Garden of the Hesperides</b> and the struggle of Heracles with Antaeus nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/lixus">Lixus</a></li></ul></section>
